--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Attribution: BBP Pairings
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ainalrami contains work derived from BBP Pairings
(https://github.com/BieremaBoyzProgramming/bbpPairings),
Copyright 2016-2026 Jeremy Bierema, licensed under the Apache License,
Version 2.0.

No BBP Pairings source code is reproduced here. Ainalrami is written in
Elixir and BBP Pairings in C++, so nothing was copied verbatim. What was
taken is the ALGORITHM AND CONTROL FLOW of two components, read directly
from the BBP Pairings source while writing the Elixir equivalents, with
the originating file and line numbers cited inline throughout. Stating
that plainly here rather than leaving it to be inferred:

  * lib/ainalrami/pairing.ex — the FIDE Dutch-system bracket cascade,
    ported stage for stage from src/swisssystems/dutch.cpp, including the
    C1-C21 criteria encoding, the bootstrap bye-assignee matching, the
    per-bracket refinement stages and the candidate-ordering (addend)
    passes.

  * lib/ainalrami/weighted_matching.ex — the Galil/Micali/Gabow (1986)
    primal-dual maximum-weight matching algorithm, with the control flow
    read from src/matching/detail/graph.cpp, rootblossom.cpp,
    parentblossom.cpp and src/matching/computer.cpp. The underlying
    algorithm is published academic work; what is derived from BBP
    Pairings is the specific implementation structure.

Changes made relative to BBP Pairings, as required by section 4(b) of the
License: the code is a reimplementation in a different language and is not
a line-for-line translation. Ainalrami.WeightedMatching originally omitted
BBP Pairings' incremental caches (minOuterEdges, per-blossom
minOuterEdgeResistance) and rescanned instead, trading the O(n^3) bound
for a smaller and more directly verifiable translation; it now maintains
equivalent caches, arrived at independently and structured per vertex
rather than per blossom, and additionally divides all edge weights by
their greatest common divisor before solving — a step BBP Pairings has no
need of, since its weights are machine integers rather than the packed
hundred-digit criteria values Ainalrami.Pairing produces.
Ainalrami.Pairing differs in its peek/window handling and in several
places where a non-literal reading is argued in the code comments rather
than copied. Individual deviations are documented at their call sites and
in docs/engineering-log.md.

The following parts of Ainalrami are NOT derived from BBP Pairings:
lib/ainalrami/trf.ex implements FIDE's published TRF16 specification
(C.04 Annex 2) and was adapted from the author's own OpenPairings
project; lib/ainalrami/sequence.ex implements Article 4 of C.04.3 (2026)
directly from the regulations; lib/ainalrami/cli.ex, generator.ex,
matching.ex and log.ex are original work.

